        why: "Every one of these is a failure this environment has actually seen: reaching for a tool that is not installed, or piping a table into a line-oriented filter and getting nothing."
        verify: [{kind: "prose"}]
        source: "prds/06-help/05-agent-interface/prd.md"
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ config
    {
        cmd: "help"
        title: "Read the manual for this environment"
        use: "Run `help` alone for the topics and the handful of keys worth knowing first. `help <topic>` gives that topic as a nushell table, so `help find | where key =~ ctrl` composes; `help <entry>` gives one entry in full, with its why and its source PRD. A name this manual does not document but the shell does is handed to nushell's own `std/help` untouched, which is what keeps every `<cmd> --help` working. Whatever is left is searched across the manual, and if that finds nothing the search falls through to `std/help`'s own, so `help <nu-word>` still lands somewhere. `help --all` prints everything."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 1
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["help --fuzzy", "help --check", "help --json"]
        why: "Nushell sanctions overriding `help`, which is the constraint the delegation exists for: this command also receives every `<anything> --help` typed in the shell, so it cannot be a manual that happens to be called `help`. Where a name is BOTH a nushell command and an entry here — `ls` is the live example — the manual wins, and winning costs you nothing: a `command` entry's detail ends with that command's own `std/help` output, and `help --entry`, `help --topic` and `help --delegate` address either side explicitly when the name is ambiguous."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "help"}]
        source: "prds/06-help/02-help-command/prd.md"
    }
    {
        cmd: "docs"
        title: "Grep the manual's prose and open it in the editor"
        use: "`docs` — or `?`, which is the same command — opens a picker over every LINE of this manual's markdown: the guide, the reference, and the internals notes that hold the constraints the configuration is built around. Type a phrase you half-remember (`OSC 52`, `parse time`, `capsule`); enter opens that file in `$EDITOR` at that line. `help` addresses entries by name, `docs` addresses sentences."
        topic: "config"
        task: "maintaining"
        step: 2
        does: "search-manual"
        mode: "shell"
        also: ["?", "help", "help --fuzzy"]
        why: "The manual was a documentation site that had to be built and served before it could be read, which meant it was not read. Markdown under `~/.config/nushell/help/manual`, `rg` and one television channel are the same search with nothing to start — and the file it lands you in is the file you edit."
        verify: [{kind: "command", name: "docs"}]
